<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p>Modern biological studies often measure thousands of genes,
  metabolites, or microbial taxa in each sample. Turning those
  measurements into defensible results requires several linked steps:
  fitting a statistical model, checking the fitted object, extracting
  comparable tables, and producing figures that can be revised for
  publication. The individual statistical methods are well implemented
  in mature R packages, but their inputs, object structures, and output
  conventions differ. Analysts therefore spend substantial effort moving
  results between packages, matching sample metadata, renaming columns,
  and redrawing similar figures.</p>
  <p><monospace>bioRtools</monospace>
  (<xref alt="LI, 2026" rid="ref-biortools2026" ref-type="bibr">LI,
  2026</xref>) is an R package
  (<xref alt="R Core Team, 2026" rid="ref-RCore2026" ref-type="bibr">R
  Core Team, 2026</xref>) that provides a shared analysis-to-figure
  layer for these workflows. It calls established engines such as DESeq2
  (<xref alt="Love et al., 2014" rid="ref-love2014deseq2" ref-type="bibr">Love
  et al., 2014</xref>), edgeR
  (<xref alt="Robinson et al., 2010" rid="ref-robinson2010edger" ref-type="bibr">Robinson
  et al., 2010</xref>), vegan
  (<xref alt="Oksanen et al., 2025" rid="ref-oksanen2025vegan" ref-type="bibr">Oksanen
  et al., 2025</xref>), mixOmics
  (<xref alt="Rohart et al., 2017" rid="ref-rohart2017mixomics" ref-type="bibr">Rohart
  et al., 2017</xref>), and ropls
  (<xref alt="Thévenot et al., 2015" rid="ref-thevenot2015ropls" ref-type="bibr">Thévenot
  et al., 2015</xref>) rather than reimplementing their statistical
  methods. Each core analysis returns the native model together with
  named result tables, editable <monospace>ggplot2</monospace> figures
  (<xref alt="Wickham, 2016" rid="ref-wickham2016ggplot2" ref-type="bibr">Wickham,
  2016</xref>), and provenance describing the input dimensions, engine
  versions, function call, and seed. The package covers differential
  expression and abundance analysis, multivariate ordination and
  classification, diversity analysis, enrichment, and common genomics
  figures. It is designed for scripted, local, and reproducible analysis
  rather than as a replacement for the underlying statistical
  engines.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p>The target users are R-using researchers, students, and
  bioinformatics support staff who repeatedly analyze plant, microbial,
  or other omics datasets. In practice, a single project may combine
  DESeq2 for count data, vegan for community ecology, mixOmics or ropls
  for multivariate metabolomics, and <monospace>ggplot2</monospace> for
  final figures. Each package uses a different object model and
  different conventions for sample identifiers, feature identifiers,
  missing values, and result extraction. A seemingly simple error, such
  as metadata sorted in a different order from the response matrix, can
  silently change a test result.</p>
  <p>Existing packages solve important parts of this problem, but they
  leave a gap between a fitted model and a reusable analysis product.
  <monospace>bioRtools</monospace> addresses that gap by enforcing a
  common result contract, checking sample alignment before model
  fitting, preserving the native statistical object, and returning the
  same table and plot vocabulary across methods. The contract is
  intended to make downstream code predictable without hiding the
  statistical engine. A thin syntax wrapper would not be sufficient: the
  research value lies in the combination of identity checks, stable
  table semantics, provenance, and composable figures across
  methods.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="state-of-the-field">
  <title>State of the field</title>
  <p>Several research-software projects make biological analysis more
  accessible. BLIT integrates command-line bioinformatics tools into R
  workflows
  (<xref alt="Ding et al., 2026" rid="ref-ding2026blit" ref-type="bibr">Ding
  et al., 2026</xref>). cOmicsArt provides a customizable environment
  for omics analysis and reporting
  (<xref alt="Seep et al., 2025" rid="ref-seep2025comicsart" ref-type="bibr">Seep
  et al., 2025</xref>). TBtools offers an interactive toolkit for large
  biological datasets
  (<xref alt="C. Chen et al., 2020" rid="ref-chen2020tbtools" ref-type="bibr">C.
  Chen et al., 2020</xref>). Hiplot and ImageGP provide web-based
  services for publication-ready biomedical and scientific visualization
  (<xref alt="T. Chen et al., 2022" rid="ref-chen2022imagegp" ref-type="bibr">T.
  Chen et al., 2022</xref>;
  <xref alt="Li et al., 2022" rid="ref-li2022hiplot" ref-type="bibr">Li
  et al., 2022</xref>).</p>
  <p>These tools differ from <monospace>bioRtools</monospace> in
  architecture and intended integration. Web services and graphical
  toolkits are convenient for interactive use but are not primarily R
  libraries for scripted model fitting. BLIT connects command-line
  programs to R but does not define a shared result contract across
  statistical models. Contributing a small feature to one of these
  projects would not provide the cross-engine behavior needed here.
  <monospace>bioRtools</monospace> was therefore built as an R-native
  layer that delegates estimation to established engines, keeps their
  objects available for inspection, and standardizes the surrounding
  analysis and figure interface. This complements rather than competes
  with the underlying methods and visualization platforms.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="software-design">
  <title>Software design</title>
  <p>The central design decision is to separate three outputs that are
  often mixed inside ad hoc scripts. A
  <monospace>bioRtools_fit</monospace> object contains
  <monospace>model</monospace>, the untouched object returned by the
  statistical engine; <monospace>tables</monospace>, a named list of
  analysis-ready data frames; <monospace>plots</monospace>, a named list
  of editable <monospace>ggplot2</monospace> objects; and
  <monospace>provenance</monospace>, which records the analysis type,
  engine and package versions, R version, input dimensions, originating
  call, and optional seed. Historical list elements remain available for
  backward compatibility, while <monospace>print()</monospace>,
  <monospace>summary()</monospace>, and
  <monospace>as_tibble()</monospace> provide predictable inspection and
  extraction. The package is released under the MIT license.</p>
  <p>Sample identity is treated as part of the statistical model, not as
  a formatting detail. The multivariate workflows resolve explicit
  sample-ID columns or non-default row names, require complete and
  unique identifiers, and reorder metadata to the response before
  fitting. Missing, extra, or duplicated identifiers produce an error
  instead of a silent positional match. This alignment layer is shared
  by principal component analysis, principal coordinate analysis,
  redundancy analysis, PERMANOVA, sparse PLS-DA, and OPLS-DA.</p>
  <p>The package also separates core dependencies from optional engines.
  Core workflows use a comparatively small set of imports; packages
  needed for specific methods or visualizations are checked at call time
  and listed as suggested dependencies. The trade-off is that some
  functions require an additional installation, but users are not forced
  to install every supported omics engine. The public vignettes exercise
  microbiome and metabolomics workflows with
  <monospace>mia::GlobalPatterns</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Caporaso et al., 2011" rid="ref-caporaso2011globalpatterns" ref-type="bibr">Caporaso
  et al., 2011</xref>) and <monospace>mixOmics::nutrimouse</monospace>
  (<xref alt="Martin et al., 2007" rid="ref-martin2007nutrimouse" ref-type="bibr">Martin
  et al., 2007</xref>), respectively. Tests compare outputs with the
  native engines and cover metadata shuffling, duplicate and missing
  identifiers, adjusted-P preservation, and end-to-end public-data
  workflows.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research impact statement</title>
  <p><monospace>bioRtools</monospace> has been publicly developed on
  GitHub since 28 July 2025. At the time of submission, the repository
  contains more than 300 commits distributed over 15 months, more than
  15 version tags, a maintained changelog, automated tests,
  continuous-integration checks, reference documentation, and public
  vignettes. This history shows iterative development rather than a
  one-time release.</p>
  <p>The software is also used in the author’s active research program.
  A local audit found 944 R, R Markdown, and Quarto files across 22
  research projects calling exported <monospace>bioRtools</monospace>
  functions. These projects include soybean pathogen pangenome and
  effector analyses, soybean GWAS and NLR work, peanut BSA and RNA-seq
  analyses, rice metagenome analysis, and assembly-benchmarking studies.
  That use has directly motivated the sample-alignment checks,
  preservation of unavailable adjusted P values, optional-dependency
  guards, and reproducible result contract described above. External
  peer-reviewed citations have not yet accrued, so this submission
  relies on documented developer use and reproducible public-data
  examples rather than claiming a mature external user community.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="ai-usage-disclosure">
  <title>AI usage disclosure</title>
  <p>Generative AI assistance was used during software development,
  documentation, and manuscript preparation. Git commit trailers record
  Anthropic Claude assistance on 394 commits between 9 February and 29
  August 2026, principally for code generation and refactoring, test
  scaffolding, documentation, and debugging; the exact Claude model
  version was not recorded. OpenAI Codex, based on the GPT-5 family, was
  used in sessions on 28-30 October 2026 to audit the package, reduce
  hard dependencies, repair tests and package APIs, develop the shared
  result contract, and draft and edit this paper.</p>
  <p>The human author reviewed and validated all AI-assisted outputs,
  ran the test and package-check suites, and retained responsibility for
  problem framing, statistical scope, API and architecture decisions,
  and final acceptance. All changes were version-controlled and tested
  before release. No AI system is listed as an author.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="acknowledgements">
  <title>Acknowledgements</title>
  <p>The author thanks the R and Bioconductor communities and the
  maintainers of the statistical engines integrated by
  <monospace>bioRtools</monospace>. No specific funding was received for
  this work. The author declares no competing interests.</p>
</sec>
</body>
